    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.db06ebbbe7fce8dc52f6",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Grafana 11 adds the ability to color full table rows using the **Colored background** cell type of the table visualization. When you configure fields in a table to use this cell type, an option to apply the color of the cell to the entire row becomes available.",
      "headingPath": [
        "What’s new in Grafana v11.0",
        "Dashboards and visualizations",
        "Colored table rows with conditional formatting"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "718032fb1fcee4d964da6aa4e9da332a64bf509a",
        "sha256": "4ac4a4d84503349934037643cc351a7722532a4c4189aab283838810ba2f6731",
        "lineStart": 223,
        "lineEnd": 223,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md#L223-L223"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.a059a28f2df493a502c3",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "This feature is useful for a wide variety of use cases including mapping status fields to colors (for example, `info`, `debug`, `warning`) and allowing rows to be colored based on threshold values. This is one of the first steps in making formatting tables more seamless, and allows for quick scanning of data using the table visualization.",
      "headingPath": [
        "What’s new in Grafana v11.0",
        "Dashboards and visualizations",
        "Colored table rows with conditional formatting"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "718032fb1fcee4d964da6aa4e9da332a64bf509a",
        "sha256": "4ac4a4d84503349934037643cc351a7722532a4c4189aab283838810ba2f6731",
        "lineStart": 227,
        "lineEnd": 227,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md#L227-L227"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.39673b67cf4f413ccb26",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "You now have the ability to customize specific colors for individual thresholds when using the **Config from query results** transformer. Previously, when you added multiple thresholds, they all defaulted to the same color, red. With this addition, you gain the flexibility to assign distinct colors to each threshold.",
      "headingPath": [
        "What’s new in Grafana v11.0",
        "Dashboards and visualizations",
        "Set threshold colors in the Config from query transformation"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "718032fb1fcee4d964da6aa4e9da332a64bf509a",
        "sha256": "4ac4a4d84503349934037643cc351a7722532a4c4189aab283838810ba2f6731",
        "lineStart": 239,
        "lineEnd": 239,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md#L239-L239"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.ece1590718ff51418c1d",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "This update to the **Filter data by values** transformation simplifies data filtering by enabling partial string matching on field values thanks to two new matchers: **Contains substring** and **Does not contain substring**. With the substring matcher built into the **Filter data by values** transformation, you can efficiently filter large datasets, displaying relevant information with speed and precision. Whether you're searching for keywords, product names, or user IDs, this feature streamlines the process, saving time and effort while ensuring accurate data output.",
      "headingPath": [
        "What’s new in Grafana v11.0",
        "Dashboards and visualizations",
        "Substring matcher added to the Filter by value transformation"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "718032fb1fcee4d964da6aa4e9da332a64bf509a",
        "sha256": "4ac4a4d84503349934037643cc351a7722532a4c4189aab283838810ba2f6731",
        "lineStart": 249,
        "lineEnd": 249,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md#L249-L249"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.3d13ccd3206938bfa4e6",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "In the **Filter data by values** transformation, simply add a condition, choose a field, choose your matcher, and then input the string to match against.",
      "headingPath": [
        "What’s new in Grafana v11.0",
        "Dashboards and visualizations",
        "Substring matcher added to the Filter by value transformation"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/whatsnew/whats-new-in-v11-0.md",
